WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.200
Making a movie might seem like relatively&nbsp;
harmless make-believe most of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.440
but sometimes those fake action&nbsp;
scenes have painful consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.829
From action stars to comedy legends, all these&nbsp;
actors got knocked out while making a movie.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.240
Few actors have been bonked and bruised&nbsp;
in the name of cinema quite as often or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.160
as famously as Jackie Chan. He broke his eye&nbsp;
socket while shooting 1978's "Drunken Master."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.360
He dislocated his pelvis and sustained second&nbsp;
degree burns and spinal damage while shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.640
the explosive pole-slide conclusion of "Police&nbsp;
Story." He even cracked his skull open while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:43.200
shooting 1986's "Armor of God," arguably the most&nbsp;
serious and squirm-inducing injury in his career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.960
That particular injury, in fact,&nbsp;
is so famous that Chan has invited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.920
other celebrities to occasionally feel the&nbsp;
hole where a part of his skull used to be.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.880
According to his autobiography, "I Am Jackie&nbsp;
Chan: My Life in Action," Chan was also knocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.720
unconscious while filming the 1976 movie "Hand&nbsp;
of Death." As Chan tells it, he hit his head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.520
after a stunt that involved a particularly&nbsp;
daring fall. Despite the pain of the impact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.960
he immediately performed the stunt again to get&nbsp;
the shot, before finally passing out. Supposedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.840
director John Woo was convinced that Chan was&nbsp;
going to die until the actor woke up an hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.360
later. Unfortunately, this would prove to be his&nbsp;
first major concussion in a career full of them.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.520
Frankly, it's a miracle that the legendary Buster&nbsp;
Keaton made it out of the movie-making business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.640
in (mostly) one piece. Heck, the man broke his&nbsp;
ankle while goofing off on a moving staircase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.200
during the shoot of "The Electric House." And&nbsp;
according to Marion Meade's Keaton biography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.480
"Cut to the Chase," the silent star&nbsp;
even fractured his neck during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.200
filming of "Sherlock Jr" when a geyser&nbsp;
from a water tower knocked him flat.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.760
Keaton, a genius of both physical comedy&nbsp;
and death-defying action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.960
famously never refused a stunt, however&nbsp;
dangerous. In 1926's "The General," Keaton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.000
re-telling of the true Civil War story of&nbsp;
a runaway locomotive stolen by Union spies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.040
Keaton was knocked unconscious&nbsp;
when he was standing too close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.560
to a Civil War replica cannon that fired&nbsp;
prematurely. That's commitment to craft.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.840
In the second cinematic outing of Jason Bourne,&nbsp;
"The Bourne Supremacy," everyone's favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:21.840
amnesiac CIA assassin has his peaceful life&nbsp;
threatened by a conspiracy involving Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.080
documents, which naturally has something to&nbsp;
do with the nefarious Operation Treadstone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.840
Bourne's covert adventure takes&nbsp;
him to an airport in Naples,&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.600
where he allows himself to get spotted&nbsp;
by security and taken into custody.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.880
During his interrogation, he overpowers CIA&nbsp;
officer John Nevins to get his SIM card.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.720
As director Paul Greengrass explains&nbsp;
on the film's director's commentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.480
the execution of the fight scene&nbsp;
didn't quite go as planned. Reportedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.800
Bourne star Matt Damon's flying fists&nbsp;
actually came into contact with Tim Griffin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.560
the actor playing Nevins,&nbsp;
accidentally knocking him out cold.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.480
While Greengrass' infamous shaky&nbsp;
cam makes the hit hard to catch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.120
if you have the luxury of replaying the punch&nbsp;
frame-by-frame, you'll indeed be treated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.160
a high-speed smack that looks like it hurt big&nbsp;
time. To his credit, from what we see on-screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.480
Damon doesn't break character. Hey,&nbsp;
remind us not to mess with Matt Damon.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.040
What if "Death Race 2000" was a whimsical&nbsp;
slapstick comedy? Well, good news, that movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.480
exists, and it's called "The Great Race." Blake&nbsp;
Edwards' 1965 film tells the unapologetically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.280
capitalistic tale of professional daredevil&nbsp;
the Great Leslie, who convinces automotive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.320
manufacturers that a race from New York to Paris&nbsp;
will increase car sales. The Great Leslie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.000
arch-nemesis — the mustachioed, black-suited&nbsp;
Professor Fate — vows to vanquish his blue-eyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.280
foe by way of a car of his own invention that&nbsp;
features, among other things, hydraulic stilts.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.840
"The Great Race" is the proud recipient of a&nbsp;
couple of cinematic superlatives, including what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:56.080
a 1965 edition of LIFE Magazine dubbed as the&nbsp;
"greatest pie fight ever." The scene featured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.640
4,000 cream pies on one massive set, and was&nbsp;
shot over five days. As fun as the ordeal sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.040
things steadily took a turn for the dangerous&nbsp;
as the shoot wore on. In the LIFE article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.160
star Jack Lemmon, who played Professor Fate,&nbsp;
claimed that he got knocked out a couple of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.640
noting that getting smacked with a high flying&nbsp;
pie feels, quote, "like a ton of cement."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.560
The same article describes how, at one&nbsp;
point, actress Natalie Wood choked briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.560
when a pie flew directly into her mouth.&nbsp;
Who knew pies could be so dangerous?

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.280
"Bad Boys" is a seminal&nbsp;
piece of 1990s action cinema,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.160
as well as a testament to the on-screen power&nbsp;
of both Will Smith and director Michael Bay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.920
The film tells the story of two Miami&nbsp;
cops — one a family man, the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.280
a womanizer — who have 72 hours to reclaim&nbsp;
heroin stolen from their station's evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.360
storage locker. Actress Téa Leoni was along for the ride as Julie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.320
the sole witness to a murder and someone who&nbsp;
requires the protection of the title characters.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.480
During the filming, Leoni was accidentally&nbsp;
knocked unconscious by co-star Martin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:01.200
Lawrence's stunt double. As the actress&nbsp;
explained to Movieline in a 2000 interview:

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.200
"It was the AK-47 under the jaw that&nbsp;
got me. I wasn't on the proper mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.160
when the stunt guy hit me with&nbsp;
it. My legs went over my head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.080
and I landed flat on my back. Didn't&nbsp;
have much memory at that point."

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.040
As she tells it, Bay, quote, "freaked out" about&nbsp;
her ability to finish the film. Leoni was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.680
to pull through, and became a part of action&nbsp;
movie history, with the injuries to prove it.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.360
"Bloodsport" is a film that could easily&nbsp;
be re-titled "Jean-Claude Van Damme vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.680
the World." The film is a delightfully&nbsp;
flagrant excuse to showcase the actor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.040
talents for taking and dishing out beatings.&nbsp;
Van Damme plays Dux, a skilled fighter invited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.320
to participate in an illegal martial arts&nbsp;
tournament in Hong Kong known as the Kumite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.880
Determined to bring honor to his state-side&nbsp;
father figure, Dux accepts the challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.200
and proceeds along the long and bloody&nbsp;
journey of beating up the best of the best.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.840
One of "the best" is Sadiq Hossein, played&nbsp;
by Bernard Mariano. During their brawl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.160
Hossein refuses to stay down and lurches&nbsp;
forward from his defeated position on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.600
floor to challenge Dux for a second go-round. The&nbsp;
script called for Dux to land a definitive punch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.800
to keep Hossein down for good. Unfortunately&nbsp;
for Mariano, Van Damme's elbow actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.520
contact with his jaw, which you can see for&nbsp;
yourself in the final film. Mariano later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.160
told the South China Morning Post that the blood&nbsp;
you see in the scene isn't movie magic, either.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.680
One of the most memorable scenes in Rob Reiner's&nbsp;
fairy tale comedy classic "The Princess Bride"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.000
takes place when the newly reunited Westley&nbsp;
and Princess Buttercup make their way through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.000
the treacherous Fire Swamp, which&nbsp;
is exactly what it sounds like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.560
Unfortunately, the pair's happy reunion&nbsp;
is short-lived. They survive the mythical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.360
creatures known as Rodents of Unusual Size&nbsp;
and even pull their way out of quicksand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.360
only to fall prey to the ambush of the&nbsp;
dastardly Prince Humperdinck. When it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.560
came time to film the shot where the prince's&nbsp;
right-hand man, Count Rugen, slugs the injured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.520
Westley with the butt of his sword, the blow&nbsp;
didn't look quite convincing enough on camera.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.880
In his memoir "As You Wish," Westley actor&nbsp;
Cary Elwes recalled that the Reiner solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.520
to the problem of making the hit look real&nbsp;
made him quite literally see stars. As he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.760
explained in the book, the camera angle meant&nbsp;
that a fake strike just didn't look right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.240
so he told Count Rugen actor Christopher&nbsp;
Guest to just, quote, "hit me hard."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.240
Guest landed a blow with the butt of his&nbsp;
sword, and Elwes woke up in the hospital.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:27.600
Released 16 years after "Rocky V," Rocky Balboa"&nbsp;
is the sixth entry in the saga of underdog boxer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.400
Rocky Balboa, and catches up with the aging&nbsp;
Philadelphia prize fighter in retirement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.280
He's in his 60s, widowed, and has a strained&nbsp;
relationship with his son. When a simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.760
predicts that superstar professional&nbsp;
boxer Mason Dixon would lose to Rocky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.360
the undefeated Dixon throws down the gauntlet in&nbsp;
the hopes of finally fighting a true contender.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.120
To everyone's surprise, Rocky&nbsp;
agrees and comes out of retirement.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.760
As reported by The Guardian, "Rocky Balboa"&nbsp;
star Sylvester Stallone was actually knocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.280
unconscious during the shoot, and the crew&nbsp;
kept filming because they thought it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.440
all part of the actor's performance in his&nbsp;
most famous role. According to the report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.400
Mason Dixon actor Antonio Tarver accidentally&nbsp;
punched Stallone in the head while shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.440
a fight scene in a Las Vegas ring, where&nbsp;
the 59-year-old actor promptly collapsed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.840
After the crew realized that no, Stallone&nbsp;
was not acting, they rallied emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.640
services to tend to him. He was rushed to the&nbsp;
hospital and thankfully made a full recovery.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.800
Ryan Coogler's "Rocky" spinoff movie "Creed"&nbsp;
sees former World Heavyweight Champion Rocky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:34.480
Balboa serving as both mentor and trainer to&nbsp;
Adonis Johnson, the son of Balboa's late friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.840
and former boxing rival, Apollo Creed. Somewhat&nbsp;
appropriately for a film intent on revisiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.200
old ground, Jordan partook in the time honored&nbsp;
'Rocky' tradition of getting laid out for real.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:46.720
"Now, you wanted this?"
"Let's go coach."&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:47.760
"I want you to make it happen."

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.000
A behind-the-scenes tweet by none other&nbsp;
than Stallone himself showed Jordan taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:57.200
a mean right hook from his co-star, very&nbsp;
real professional fighter Tony Bellew...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:01.280
and falling flat on the floor. On "The Graham&nbsp;
Norton Show," Jordan recalled that the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:06.240
punch was orchestrated by Stallone to achieve&nbsp;
a convincing slow-motion shot. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.640
the first time they did the real punch, Bellew's&nbsp;
glove got in the way of the camera, so they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.760
to do another take. And it's this second hit&nbsp;
that dropped Jordan like a sack of potatoes.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.320
While the actor assured the talk show host that&nbsp;
he wasn't "technically" unconscious, he admits,

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.120
"I was definitely seeing stars. It was&nbsp;
definitely not. I was not all there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.280
Smelling the canvas and&nbsp;
everything, I'll never forget it."

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.720
While promoting "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.&nbsp;
2" on "Live with Kelly and Ryan," Chris Pratt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.480
relayed the details of a stunt-gone-wrong in&nbsp;
his latest outing as Marvel's Star-Lord. Pratt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.840
explained that he got knocked out while he and an&nbsp;
unspecified co-star were filming some wirework.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.720
As Pratt describes in the interview, the accident&nbsp;
was the result of the stunt happening too fast.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.560
According to the star, he was trying to&nbsp;
land on his shoulder when he hit his head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.440
on a protective mat instead. Thankfully,&nbsp;
the moment of unconsciousness was brief.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:03.120
"And I realized if I didn't do the scene I&nbsp;
was going to have to do the stunt again."

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.560
Given the amount of rig and wirework&nbsp;
involved in the "Guardians" sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.080
we're not even going to try and guess&nbsp;
which specific scene was being filmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.600
when Pratt came crashing down. Maybe&nbsp;
one day he'll tell the whole story.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.880
Wes Craven's 2005 airborne thriller&nbsp;
"Red Eye" tells the story of Lisa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.280
a woman kidnapped by a stranger on a&nbsp;
routine commercial flight bound for Miami.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.360
With her father's life in the balance,&nbsp;
Lisa finds herself dragged, unwillingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.520
into assisting her menacing captor&nbsp;
in the assassination of a politician.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:36.720
In one scene, stars Cillian Murphy and Rachel&nbsp;
McAdams duke it out in an airplane bathroom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.600
At the beginning of the confrontation, Murphy&nbsp;
rushes at McAdams, pinning her against a wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:44.960
However, while filming the scene,&nbsp;
it seems Murphy miscalculated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.120
and McAdams hit her head for real and&nbsp;
supposedly lost consciousness for a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:53.600
The incident is included in the film's gag&nbsp;
reel, so clearly there were no hard feelings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:56.960
If you look closely during the actual&nbsp;
scene as it appears in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.000
you can catch Murphy's hand protecting&nbsp;
McAdams' head from future injury. How sweet.

